WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.510 align:start position:0%
 
we talked to the health secretary matt  hancock about the uk's borders amid  reports the government could tighten  rules for  arrivals from abroad it's something  labour's shadow home secretary nick  thomas simmons has some opinions on  and he joins us now from south wales  good to see you what should happen when  when it comes to border control do you  think  well good morning stephen and obviously  please do pass my best wishes to sophie  for a swift  recovery nobody could say  that the situation at our borders is  satisfactory or that there is sufficient  protection in place for our people  against the different strains that could  be coming from around the world  so we need now to put additional  protections  in place we do need that effective  border testing regime  we also need an effective quarantining  regime which we've not  had the government did not  introduce a comprehensive quarantining  regime until june last year this  government was an  international outlier if you go back to  may last year  it was only the uk iran luxembourg and  the u.s virgin islands that had no  travel protections in place  it was too slow it was put into place in  june it isn't effective at the moment  we need that additional quarantining  protection  in place i would support for example the  introduction of the use of hotels  but above all we need a comprehensive  system and plan from the government  and stop moving from one chaotic  situation to another well hotels i mean  it's been it's been talked about before  and other countries are doing it are you  saying that everyone who arrives in the  uk  should be put into a hotel and forcibly  quarantined for  i don't know what would it be 10 10 days  or so  it does need to be a comprehensive  system clearly you would have to have  a very different system for hauliers for  lorries crossing the english channel  that would have to be obviously very  different  but in terms of uh travelers uh arriving  in the united kingdom you would need to  have that comprehensive system  let me just say why this is so important  because the government introduced  a quarantining system far too slowly in  june of last year  the government's own figures show that  between june of last year  and september around about two million  people  had their passenger locator forms their  details if you like  spot checked by border force at the  border  only around three percent of those were  successfully contacted to ensure they  were quarantined now that just  isn't good enough it isn't effective the  government hasn't had a grip on this  situation throughout  we desperately need them to get a grip  and have that comprehensive plan in  place i mean  even the meeting tomorrow and tuesday  that they're talking about having  should have happened on thursday even  that is too slow  this can't continue they need to have  that meeting  but forgive me it's all well and good to  talk about having a comprehensive  system in place and on the surface a lot  of people would agree with it but it's  all about the nuts and bolts  isn't it it's about what that system  actually is and  how you implement it back back to that  dreaded word  of logistics which is going to be  problematic with this so what exactly  would you do if you were in government  well the labour government would have  taken a very  different approach to the setup of the  quarantining system in the first place  the government's isolation assurance  service decided to  outsource this system to a company  called  sitl the government's own target of  contacting  20 of those coming into the united  kingdom hasn't been  met i mean i indicated the figures in my  last answer it's nowhere near that  so we would actually have used a far  more effective  system wouldn't have outsourced it in  the way that the government has  and we would have acted more quickly  i've been raising the issue of border  protections  from last april in april in june in  september  time and time again i've been raising  this issue  and the government has been but forgive  me forgive me  i mean we are where we are today  on the 24th of january so what would you  do moving forward  it's no good deciding what we what you  would have done it's what what would you  do from here on in  well i think the government does now  need to introduce quarantining hotels as  i've  already indicated the government also  then  needs to have that effective testing  system in place  and needs to avoid the situation  that we've seen over the weekend of  those crowds and crowds of people  at heathrow airport and that's happening  because the government does not have  this strategy in place i think by  putting in place that system  around hotels we've seen it work well in  australia for example  then that would be a step forward but i  come back to the central point  we can't keep moving chaotically  from one situation to another we need  that comprehensive system it needs to be  put in place  what about the restrictions that we've  got in place at the moment because  there's a lot of concern about the new  variant concern that it may be  and it is a may at the moment but it may  be more deadly we certainly know it's  more  transmissible the current lockdown rules  are  looser a little bit than they were in  the summit  does that need to change well look we  called for  a lockdown the government then for  england introduced that lockdown which  they  uh in my view had to do could we do  more yes of course we could do more  we've already been talking throughout  this interview  about the situation at the border and  i've indicated that that absolutely  needs to be tightened up  but there's also an issue around  messaging and the government getting its  messaging right  you know we had last summer for example  the so-called back to work message  trying to get people back  into the office and yet we're seeing  only in the last couple of days  in the government's own agencies take  the dvla in swansea here in wales from  where i'm  speaking to you where we've had an  outbreak in a workplace so we need  not only to make sure we get this  borders issue right which is absolutely  vital particularly for the strains that  are coming from abroad  but government messaging needs to be  more consistent and effective as well  can we just turn our attention to to the  state of the union because you've seen  the sunday times and that yougov poll  today look if as expected  the smp does as well in the elections in  may  as they're talking about they're on  about possibly getting a total of  70 seats which i think is more than half  of the number of seats in the  in the scottish parliament would that  give them a referendum a mandate for a  second referendum and would labor  support that  even if it didn't have westminster  approval  i think it would be a profound mistake  to take  the election of may in scotland or  indeed  any other part of the united kingdom for  granted and if any party  is taking votes for granted then they  clearly shouldn't be doing that we have  a very clear position going into those  elections in scotland  uh against the idea of a second  independence referendum and i  and it is and it is frustrating at the  moment i have to say because at the  moment  we are in the midst of this pandemic it  is this pandemic  and working together across the united  kingdom to try to  do our best to save life that should be  the absolute focus at the moment  not this constitutional issue okay nick  thomas simmons good to talk to you thank  you

